{"qid": "gf-13", "paper_id": "2001.06899", "answer": true, "unanimous": true, "quote": "Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes.", "all_quotes": ["Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes.", "The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets.", "Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40\\hskip-0.80002pt GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection ( $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ ) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events."], "answers": ["The MET cut of 40 GeV is used to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes, resulting in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a top quark rejection factor of approximately 4.8.", "The MET cut of 40 GeV is used to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. It results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a top quark rejection factor of approximately 4.8.", "The text mentions a $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection cut of 40 GeV, but does not provide a reason for choosing this value.", "The text mentions a MET cut of 40 GeV in the context of missing transverse momentum selection efficiency, but does not provide a reason for choosing 40 GeV.", "The text mentions a missing transverse momentum selection efficiency with a cut of $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$, but does not explain why 40 GeV was chosen.", "The text mentions that the missing transverse momentum (MET) is required to be less than 40 GeV to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. It also states that this requirement results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a top quark rejection factor of approximately 4.8."], "votes": {"False": 24, "True": 6}, "escalated": false, "sections_read": [" + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "Appendix B Measured differential cross section ratios", "Cross section ratio measurements + Analysis strategy + $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ +HF jets event tagging efficiency", "Estimation of the event yields", "Object reconstruct