ficiency of ${\\\\approx}80\\\\%$ with the $\\\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\\\mathup{{\\\\overline{{{\\\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\\\approx}4.8$ .\"\n  ],\n  \"answer\": \"The text mentions that the missing transverse momentum (MET) is required to be less than 40 GeV to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. It also states that this requirement results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a top quark rejection factor of approximately 4.8.\",\n  \"found\": true\n}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-13", "answer": true, "quote": "Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The MET cut of 40 GeV is used to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. It results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a top quark rejection factor of approximately 4.8.", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The text touches on the MET cut and its purpose, but does not explicitly state why 40 GeV was chosen.\",\n  \"quotes\": [\n    \"Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}$ , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .\"\n  